# What the image build does not need. Render sends the repository as the build context unless
# this file says otherwise, and two of these directories are larger than everything the image
# actually contains.
#
# `.venv` and `node_modules` are the two that matter: 131 MB and 155 MB of platform-specific
# dependencies that the image installs its own copies of, into a different Python and a
# different libc. Sending them would slow every build and could shadow the correct ones.
.venv/
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/

# **Credentials, and this is the one that matters most.** `backend/.env` holds the model key
# and the mailbox password, and `COPY backend/` would bake it into a layer. A layer keeps what
# it was given: deleting the file in a later instruction does not take it out of the image, and
# anyone who can pull the image can read it. Render passes every one of these as an environment
# variable at run time, which is the only place they should ever be.
#
# The example file stays. It contains variable names and no values, and it is how the next
# person learns what the service needs.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!backend/.env.example

# Caches and build output, none of which belongs in an image.
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.pytest_cache/
.demo/
.playwright-mcp/
backend/cache/
backend/tests/__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Repository metadata and editor state.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
**/.DS_Store

# The submission package and the deployment notes it was built from.
docs/media/
deploy/
design/
fixtures/
